ie","src":"charts","grp":"1001 Albums","list_year":null,"rank":760,"year":1994,"note":null,"total":999,"scaruffi":8.0}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Portishead's debut album was released in the summer of 1994. The following year, it earned the prestigious Mercury Prize, solidifying its critical acclaim. \"Portus Head's debut record came out on the summer of 1994, and it won the Mercury Prize the following year, and this was one of the singles.\"","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The 90's Part 2B: Front Women","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Eo32mAZd8DI"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Released in 1994, this album predated Massive Attack's *Protection* and became widely regarded as the definitive Trip Hop record, especially in the United States. Geoff Barrow infused it with \"cinematic anguish,\" creating an album characterized by dark, unhealthy sounds, all filtered through a dragging, melancholic urban atmosphere that starkly contrasted with Beth Gibbons' spectral vocals. Many critics described it as \"gothic Hip Hop\" or the soundtrack to an unreleased dark film. \"Así que en 1994 por Tisket se les aderantaron con Dami.\"","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Central Sonora","episode":"El género más OSCURO de los 90 (...que acabó en anuncios de coches)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gAEaRBEw9c"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"The stripped-down sound of Portishead's album \"Dummy\" fascinated Noel Gallagher and served as an inspiration for the vibe he aimed to create with Oasis' \"Wonderwall,\" though without venturing into trip-hop territory. \"Noll had become fascinated by the stripped-down sound of the Portushead album, Dummy, and was looking to create that vibe without going into trip-hop territory.\"","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"In the Studio with Oasis","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IhG-WJH9om8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Debut Record","body":"Portishead's debut album was released in the summer of 1994. The following year, it earned the prestigious Mercury Prize, solidifying its critical acclaim. \"Portus Head's debut record came out on the summer of 1994, and it won the Mercury Prize the following year, and this was one of the singles.\"","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The 90's Part 2B: Front Women","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Eo32mAZd8DI"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Definitive Trip Hop Soundtrack","body":"Released in 1994, this album predated Massive Attack's *Protection* and became widely regarded as the definitive Trip Hop record, especially in the United States. Geoff Barrow infused it with \"cinematic anguish,\" creating an album characterized by dark, unhealthy sounds, all filtered through a dragging, melancholic urban atmosphere that starkly contrasted with Beth Gibbons' spectral vocals. Many critics described it as \"gothic Hip Hop\" or the soundtrack to an unreleased dark film. \"Así que en 1994 por Tisket se les aderantaron con Dami.\"","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Central Sonora","episode":"El género más OSCURO de los 90 (...que acabó en anuncios de coches)","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gAEaRBEw9c"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":167617327686436,"artist":"Portishead","artist_id":189914799469949,"title":"Dummy","year":1994,"ess":1,"yt":"hd9OYCqBK88","kind":null,"book":{"id":10,"title":"Loops 1 y 2. Una historia de la música electrónica","author":"Javier Blánquez (ed.)","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/loops_timeline/"},"scene":"Trip-hop","scene_id":176775912198818,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/loops_timeline/#trip-hop","chapters":["Cap. 13: Medianoche en un mundo perfecto: abstracción hip hop y ciencia del beat a cámara lenta (1987-2002)"],"pages":[[1,238],[1,242],[1,243],[1,254],[1,450]]}],"others":[{"id":2488741442